1
00:00:47,213 --> 00:00:54,179
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

2
00:01:24,417 --> 00:01:25,376
It's me.

3
00:01:33,718 --> 00:01:35,053
What's going on this late?

4
00:01:48,733 --> 00:01:49,692
So he's dead.

5
00:02:05,041 --> 00:02:05,875
All right.

6
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:08,503
I see.

7
00:02:17,262 --> 00:02:18,221
Wait a moment.

8
00:02:30,358 --> 00:02:31,734
You got soaked in the rain.

9
00:02:43,121 --> 00:02:44,038
All right, then.

10
00:02:45,373 --> 00:02:46,291
Go get some sleep.

11
00:02:48,293 --> 00:02:49,502
A castella cake.

12
00:02:51,671 --> 00:02:53,965
He told me to buy you that.

13
00:02:57,135 --> 00:02:57,969
What?

14
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:02,348
Seo-hee's father…

15
00:03:05,643 --> 00:03:07,145
told me…

16
00:03:09,564 --> 00:03:12,775
to get you a castella cake.

17
00:03:16,112 --> 00:03:19,782
Did I hear that right?

18
00:03:19,866 --> 00:03:21,492
On your way home,

19
00:03:22,702 --> 00:03:25,288
buy a castella cake.

20
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:28,041
Shut up!

21
00:03:40,094 --> 00:03:40,929
That was

22
00:03:42,222 --> 00:03:46,392
the last thing he ever said.

23
00:03:48,728 --> 00:03:52,607
I didn't know what a castella was.

24
00:03:59,822 --> 00:04:04,327
On the day
of the March First Movement in Kyeam-ri,

25
00:04:06,663 --> 00:04:10,083
Seo-hee had a cold
and was unusually fussy that morning.

26
00:04:12,210 --> 00:04:13,628
So I asked him

27
00:04:13,711 --> 00:04:16,130
if he could stay home that day.

28
00:04:17,215 --> 00:04:18,258
Around then,

29
00:04:19,467 --> 00:04:21,469
he'd been coming home late every night.

30
00:04:23,554 --> 00:04:24,806
But he said

31
00:04:26,266 --> 00:04:29,310
he had something important to do
and had to go out.

32
00:04:31,646 --> 00:04:33,314
Just as he was leaving,

33
00:04:34,023 --> 00:04:35,650
he said to me…

34
00:04:38,695 --> 00:04:41,990
When spring comes,
let's take Seo-hee to Gyeongseong

35
00:04:43,074 --> 00:04:44,409
and have some castella.

36
00:04:45,827 --> 00:04:47,412
Castella?

37
00:04:47,495 --> 00:04:50,039
It's a sweet, soft bread.

38
00:04:51,291 --> 00:04:53,293
I'm sure you'll both like it.

39
00:04:56,587 --> 00:04:57,630
Sure.

40
00:05:19,277 --> 00:05:22,363
Thank you for passing that along.

41
00:05:47,638 --> 00:05:49,349
I knew this would happen.

42
00:07:49,927 --> 00:07:52,013
<i>- Nieun.</i>
<i>- Nieun.</i>

43
00:07:53,598 --> 00:07:55,224
<i>- A.</i>
<i>- A.</i>

44
00:07:56,476 --> 00:07:58,060
<i>- Bieub.</i>
<i>- Bieub.</i>

45
00:07:59,854 --> 00:08:01,355
<i>- I.</i>
<i>- I.</i>

46
00:08:02,190 --> 00:08:05,026
<i>- Nabi.</i>
<i>- Nabi.</i>

47
00:08:05,109 --> 00:08:06,194
A butterfly.

48
00:08:07,987 --> 00:08:08,946
Well done.

49
00:08:09,030 --> 00:08:10,156
How about we draw one?

50
00:08:10,239 --> 00:08:11,532
Yeah, give it a try.

51
00:09:07,838 --> 00:09:12,426
HEEWON

52
00:09:13,594 --> 00:09:15,888
EPISODE 7

53
00:09:37,285 --> 00:09:38,703
Keep an eye on Keum Seo-hee.

54
00:09:39,203 --> 00:09:40,538
We don't want any trouble.

55
00:09:42,790 --> 00:09:45,418
He should have been given a proper trial.

56
00:09:47,670 --> 00:09:48,504
Is he really…

57
00:09:50,715 --> 00:09:51,799
with the Gugukdan?

58
00:09:55,970 --> 00:09:58,097
You never used to ask questions like that.

59
00:09:58,180 --> 00:09:59,015
Why?

60
00:09:59,682 --> 00:10:01,809
Have you had a change of heart
since coming to Joseon?

61
00:10:04,270 --> 00:10:05,813
I told you not to show it, didn't I?

62
00:10:06,564 --> 00:10:07,940
Even with me,

63
00:10:08,983 --> 00:10:10,568
do not let it show.

64
00:10:14,864 --> 00:10:16,365
And yet look at you now.

65
00:10:18,326 --> 00:10:20,161
You look like a pathetic little kid.

66
00:10:34,634 --> 00:10:41,057
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

67
00:10:52,360 --> 00:10:53,444
Thank you.

68
00:10:59,867 --> 00:11:00,701
Good morning!

69
00:11:00,785 --> 00:11:01,702
Inspector General!

70
00:11:04,038 --> 00:11:05,414
- Good morning!
- Good morning!

71
00:11:11,879 --> 00:11:13,172
- Good morning!
- Good morning!

72
00:11:39,073 --> 00:11:40,241
The inspector general

73
00:11:41,283 --> 00:11:43,077
usually takes the elevator.

74
00:11:43,828 --> 00:11:45,538
So you won't run into him often.

75
00:12:15,484 --> 00:12:17,361
My mother was a Joseon person.

76
00:12:23,033 --> 00:12:24,243
I mean my stepmother.

77
00:12:25,828 --> 00:12:28,789
Or I suppose
I should call her my adoptive mother.

78
00:12:29,915 --> 00:12:31,917
I don't remember my birth mother.

79
00:12:33,377 --> 00:12:36,130
My father took me in as his son,

80
00:12:37,256 --> 00:12:38,424
and then married her.

81
00:12:40,342 --> 00:12:42,219
Apparently, even more so than today,

82
00:12:42,303 --> 00:12:44,472
marriages between Japanese
and Joseon people

83
00:12:44,555 --> 00:12:46,056
were encouraged back then.

84
00:12:48,976 --> 00:12:49,810
Right.

85
00:12:51,187 --> 00:12:52,563
I know that.

86
00:12:55,191 --> 00:12:57,193
We only lived together for a short time.

87
00:13:01,113 --> 00:13:03,574
But my mother taught me what love was.

88
00:13:39,318 --> 00:13:40,778
<i>She even taught me</i>

89
00:13:41,987 --> 00:13:44,490
<i>how it would feel</i>
<i>to be abandoned by that love.</i>

90
00:13:57,753 --> 00:13:59,004
Mother.

91
00:14:11,100 --> 00:14:12,226
Your mother…

92
00:14:13,978 --> 00:14:15,271
is not coming back.

93
00:14:20,109 --> 00:14:21,235
<i>My mother</i>

94
00:14:21,944 --> 00:14:24,071
<i>left us out of the blue one day.</i>

95
00:14:51,849 --> 00:14:55,811
Why are you telling me all this?

96
00:15:04,403 --> 00:15:05,779
I wanted to apologize

97
00:15:06,906 --> 00:15:08,198
for the things I said yesterday.

98
00:15:09,658 --> 00:15:11,660
<i>People like you make me sick.</i>

99
00:15:12,494 --> 00:15:14,830
People who throw words around…

100
00:15:15,873 --> 00:15:17,416
with no intention of standing by them.

101
00:15:22,046 --> 00:15:23,005
I'm sorry

102
00:15:24,673 --> 00:15:26,008
for what I said yesterday.

103
00:15:40,814 --> 00:15:41,982
My mother…

104
00:15:44,151 --> 00:15:45,611
abandoned me too.

105
00:15:47,780 --> 00:15:49,156
She married a Japanese man.

106
00:15:50,824 --> 00:15:51,742
And then,

107
00:15:53,369 --> 00:15:54,745
she died.

108
00:16:01,335 --> 00:16:03,545
But I'm not someone to pity.

109
00:16:04,088 --> 00:16:05,798
I've felt that way a few times,

110
00:16:06,924 --> 00:16:08,926
but really, I was just unlucky.

111
00:16:11,303 --> 00:16:12,304
The only thing is…

112
00:16:14,390 --> 00:16:18,143
if she was going to leave me anyway,

113
00:16:19,853 --> 00:16:23,399
I only hope she was happy with him
until the very end.

114
00:16:25,901 --> 00:16:29,029
Whatever you do, you should at least
make sure it was worth it.

115
00:16:33,659 --> 00:16:37,746
Anyway, after that,
I did my best to get by on my own.

116
00:16:39,164 --> 00:16:41,625
Well, I'd say I'm doing pretty well,
aren't I?

117
00:16:42,835 --> 00:16:45,921
I was cast out onto the streets of Jongno
without so much as a worn-out spoon,

118
00:16:46,005 --> 00:16:48,966
but I made it out alive.

119
00:16:55,305 --> 00:16:56,724
Ms. Keum,

120
00:16:59,560 --> 00:17:00,853
you're a good person.

121
00:17:04,023 --> 00:17:05,816
That's why it's hard to understand.

122
00:17:07,818 --> 00:17:08,694
He…

123
00:17:10,904 --> 00:17:12,239
is a married man.

124
00:17:14,825 --> 00:17:16,827
You said enough about that yesterday.

125
00:17:16,910 --> 00:17:18,746
Did it have to be him?

126
00:17:24,626 --> 00:17:27,212
I don't need you to understand me.

127
00:17:29,173 --> 00:17:31,300
I'll stop by the post office.

128
00:17:53,614 --> 00:17:57,201
I really hate pretending
to have an affair.

129
00:18:11,799 --> 00:18:12,800
I'm off.

130
00:18:16,887 --> 00:18:18,222
I need to ask you a favor.

131
00:18:20,724 --> 00:18:21,767
It's about Seo-hee.

132
00:18:24,144 --> 00:18:26,188
Please find a way to get her to America.

133
00:18:27,856 --> 00:18:29,274
- So-ran.
- I know.

134
00:18:29,775 --> 00:18:33,487
The United States doesn't allow
Asian immigrants right now.

135
00:18:33,570 --> 00:18:36,365
Some Joseon people manage to study there,

136
00:18:36,448 --> 00:18:38,492
but she never even went to school.

137
00:18:38,992 --> 00:18:40,994
And the Government-General
already knows about her,

138
00:18:41,078 --> 00:18:43,705
so forging her paperwork
for studying abroad wouldn't be easy.

139
00:18:43,789 --> 00:18:48,293
Yes, that's why we planned to pay her off
and settle things once this was over.

140
00:18:48,377 --> 00:18:50,420
I feel bad for lying about
sending her to America,

141
00:18:50,504 --> 00:18:51,880
but she does love money--

142
00:18:51,964 --> 00:18:53,340
Yes, that was the plan.

143
00:18:53,423 --> 00:18:55,717
We also said that
if she turned against us,

144
00:18:55,801 --> 00:18:59,555
her very existence could put us in danger.

145
00:19:01,890 --> 00:19:05,144
Still, I want to keep her alive
and get her to America.

146
00:19:05,853 --> 00:19:07,354
I want to keep my promise.

147
00:19:09,815 --> 00:19:10,691
Do you mean that?

148
00:19:11,567 --> 00:19:12,401
Yes.

149
00:19:13,485 --> 00:19:15,863
I believe you'll find a way.

150
00:19:17,531 --> 00:19:18,699
Please, I'm asking you.

151
00:19:33,213 --> 00:19:35,424
SATO, DIVISION OF REGIONAL AFFAIRS

152
00:19:37,509 --> 00:19:38,969
Excuse me.

153
00:19:39,052 --> 00:19:42,514
This was put in our mailbox by mistake.
It's not for the Foreign Affairs Division.

154
00:19:44,308 --> 00:19:45,767
Mr. Sato of Regional Affairs!

155
00:19:45,851 --> 00:19:48,520
I saw "Sato"
and put it in Foreign Affairs by mistake.

156
00:19:48,604 --> 00:19:49,688
Thank you very much.

157
00:19:49,771 --> 00:19:53,567
Sato is a common surname, and there are
several of them at the Government-General.

158
00:19:55,652 --> 00:19:56,653
INSPECTOR GENERAL

159
00:19:59,990 --> 00:20:02,034
<i>Suwon Police Station?</i>

160
00:20:02,784 --> 00:20:04,661
PERSONAL, CONFIDENTIAL
FOR THE ADDRESSEE ONLY

161
00:20:04,745 --> 00:20:08,540
TO SATO SHINICHI, THE INSPECTOR GENERAL

162
00:20:08,624 --> 00:20:12,127
<i>What could be personal and confidential</i>
<i>for the inspector general?</i>

163
00:20:12,211 --> 00:20:13,587
His name is Keum Seok-jung.

164
00:20:13,670 --> 00:20:15,672
He's from Kyeam-ri, Suwon.

165
00:20:16,673 --> 00:20:18,258
<i>Could it be about Kyeam-ri?</i>

166
00:20:18,342 --> 00:20:19,343
Kobayashi!

167
00:20:19,426 --> 00:20:20,260
Yes, sir!

168
00:20:30,854 --> 00:20:31,813
Darn it.

169
00:20:44,284 --> 00:20:48,205
Put me through to Inspector Matsui Kenji
at Suwon Police Station in Gyeonggi.

170
00:20:53,377 --> 00:20:54,753
<i>Yes, Matsui speaking.</i>

171
00:20:56,338 --> 00:20:58,423
I'm sorry to interrupt.

172
00:20:58,507 --> 00:21:01,093
I'm Sato from Regional Affairs
at the Government-General.

173
00:21:02,135 --> 00:21:05,639
The reason I'm calling is that
I received some mail you sent.

174
00:21:05,722 --> 00:21:08,308
I checked the contents,
and I don't think it was meant for me.

175
00:21:08,392 --> 00:21:09,226
Sorry?

176
00:21:10,018 --> 00:21:12,771
I sent that
to the Inspector General's Office.

177
00:21:12,854 --> 00:21:14,147
How did it end up there?

178
00:21:15,023 --> 00:21:15,857
Wait.

179
00:21:16,441 --> 00:21:18,277
Then, did you open the envelope?

180
00:21:18,860 --> 00:21:22,197
Yes, it was addressed to Mr. Sato
at the Government-General.

181
00:21:22,281 --> 00:21:24,032
The post office delivered it to me.

182
00:21:24,116 --> 00:21:25,826
My goodness, what was I thinking?

183
00:21:26,368 --> 00:21:29,830
I guess it wasn't marked as "personal" and
"confidential" for the inspector general.

184
00:21:29,913 --> 00:21:30,956
No.

185
00:21:31,039 --> 00:21:32,791
I can't believe this.

186
00:21:33,625 --> 00:21:34,459
<i>I'm sorry.</i>

187
00:21:34,543 --> 00:21:36,628
I ended up taking out what was inside.

188
00:21:36,712 --> 00:21:39,089
If you give me
a list of the documents you sent,

189
00:21:39,172 --> 00:21:42,759
I'll put everything in a new envelope and
send it to the Inspector General's Office.

190
00:21:42,843 --> 00:21:44,886
I'd really appreciate that.

191
00:21:46,763 --> 00:21:48,098
The documents I sent were…

192
00:21:51,476 --> 00:21:54,479
the report file
on the Kyeam-ri riot in 1919,

193
00:21:54,563 --> 00:21:58,191
<i>the files on four of the main suspects</i>
<i>who were persons of interest,</i>

194
00:21:58,275 --> 00:22:00,152
<i>their individual persons</i>
<i>of interest cards,</i>

195
00:22:00,235 --> 00:22:02,112
along with their court rulings,
prison records,

196
00:22:03,363 --> 00:22:04,614
and surveillance report.

197
00:22:05,741 --> 00:22:08,035
Yes, it's all here.

198
00:22:08,118 --> 00:22:10,746
I'll put everything back in place
and deliver it. Don't worry.

199
00:22:10,829 --> 00:22:13,790
And I'll make sure to mark it
"personal" and "confidential" as well.

200
00:22:14,916 --> 00:22:16,001
Thank you so much.

201
00:22:16,084 --> 00:22:16,960
All right, then.

202
00:22:17,044 --> 00:22:18,253
<i>Oh, wait a moment.</i>

203
00:22:19,087 --> 00:22:22,174
There should be
a family photo in there too.

204
00:22:23,925 --> 00:22:25,552
A family photo?

205
00:22:25,635 --> 00:22:26,553
Yes.

206
00:22:26,636 --> 00:22:29,848
There's a photo of a man
named Keum Seok-jung,

207
00:22:29,931 --> 00:22:32,100
one of the main suspects,
with his wife and child.

208
00:22:35,520 --> 00:22:36,938
Yes, I've got it right here.

209
00:22:37,022 --> 00:22:38,398
Take care, then.

210
00:22:38,482 --> 00:22:39,566
<i>Thank you.</i>

211
00:22:53,205 --> 00:22:54,414
Hello there!

212
00:22:55,123 --> 00:22:56,416
Hello.

213
00:22:59,211 --> 00:23:01,505
You must be Ms. Yamada,
the inspector general's secretary.

214
00:23:02,547 --> 00:23:03,382
Yes.

215
00:23:07,594 --> 00:23:09,054
I'm sorry.

216
00:23:09,137 --> 00:23:11,431
I must've surprised you,
acting like we knew each other.

217
00:23:12,099 --> 00:23:12,933
Sorry?

218
00:23:14,768 --> 00:23:17,896
I've heard so much about you
from Interpreter Sato.

219
00:23:17,979 --> 00:23:20,107
I felt like I already knew you.

220
00:23:20,690 --> 00:23:23,443
I'm sorry if I came on too strong.

221
00:23:25,862 --> 00:23:27,906
He's been talking about me?

222
00:23:27,989 --> 00:23:29,074
Yes.

223
00:23:29,157 --> 00:23:31,743
You wouldn't believe
how often he talks about you.

224
00:23:32,953 --> 00:23:36,123
Just the other day,
while eating the cookies you brought,

225
00:23:36,206 --> 00:23:37,958
he said, "Ms. Yamada… Ms. Ya…

226
00:23:40,961 --> 00:23:43,046
mada is…"

227
00:23:44,005 --> 00:23:45,340
Ms. Yamada what?

228
00:23:46,174 --> 00:23:47,008
Yama…

229
00:23:49,970 --> 00:23:51,430
I'm sorry.

230
00:23:52,222 --> 00:23:53,890
It was on the tip of my tongue.

231
00:23:53,974 --> 00:23:56,935
I've been so tired lately,
so I'm forgetful.

232
00:23:59,229 --> 00:24:00,772
I should eat something sweet.

233
00:24:01,356 --> 00:24:04,025
That'll help me remember…

234
00:24:04,109 --> 00:24:04,943
By any chance,

235
00:24:06,736 --> 00:24:08,238
do you like chocolate?

236
00:24:10,740 --> 00:24:11,575
Yes.

237
00:24:12,409 --> 00:24:15,120
Ms. Wada from our secretary's office
is out sick today,

238
00:24:15,203 --> 00:24:18,540
so I've been swamped by myself.

239
00:24:18,623 --> 00:24:21,668
I didn't even have time
to pick up the mail.

240
00:24:21,751 --> 00:24:24,713
I just managed to come down
after serving tea to the guests.

241
00:24:25,922 --> 00:24:27,299
That's quite a lot of mail.

242
00:24:27,382 --> 00:24:28,508
Do you want me to help you?

243
00:24:28,592 --> 00:24:30,218
No, I'm fine.

244
00:24:30,302 --> 00:24:31,470
Oh, right. You're fine.

245
00:24:33,263 --> 00:24:34,598
INSPECTOR GENERAL'S OFFICE

246
00:24:34,681 --> 00:24:36,725
We have guests inside,
so keep your voice down.

247
00:25:02,918 --> 00:25:03,752
Come in.

248
00:25:05,378 --> 00:25:06,254
Thanks.

249
00:25:16,890 --> 00:25:19,309
The new-season catalog is here.

250
00:25:20,560 --> 00:25:23,438
This one's for the inspector general…

251
00:25:26,233 --> 00:25:28,360
The chocolate is in the break room.

252
00:25:28,443 --> 00:25:30,403
One moment.

253
00:25:30,487 --> 00:25:32,447
Sure. Take your time.

254
00:25:32,531 --> 00:25:33,865
Okay.

255
00:25:40,956 --> 00:25:41,831
SUWON POLICE STATION

256
00:25:42,749 --> 00:25:43,917
GINZA BOUTIQUE

257
00:25:52,467 --> 00:25:53,510
Here you go.

258
00:25:53,593 --> 00:25:55,554
Here's chocolate.

259
00:25:55,637 --> 00:25:56,930
Thank you.

260
00:26:01,893 --> 00:26:04,813
What did Interpreter Sato say about me?

261
00:26:10,026 --> 00:26:11,152
He said…

262
00:26:14,072 --> 00:26:14,990
Ms. Yamada.

263
00:26:15,073 --> 00:26:18,285
I see you have your clothes tailored
at Ginza Boutique on Bonjeong Avenue.

264
00:26:18,368 --> 00:26:19,578
That's right.

265
00:26:20,495 --> 00:26:22,706
This outfit is from there.

266
00:26:23,456 --> 00:26:25,917
Of course.
I was thinking how pretty it was.

267
00:26:28,086 --> 00:26:31,506
Though, of course,
you look pretty in anything.

268
00:26:33,049 --> 00:26:34,843
I get that a lot.

269
00:26:36,553 --> 00:26:39,014
I'll have to go there myself
when they get new styles in.

270
00:26:40,682 --> 00:26:42,726
This is their new-season catalog.

271
00:26:42,809 --> 00:26:43,727
Really?

272
00:26:44,227 --> 00:26:46,605
Great! Let's have a look together.

273
00:26:46,688 --> 00:26:47,856
Sounds good!

274
00:26:51,318 --> 00:26:53,737
Where's the letter opener?

275
00:26:53,820 --> 00:26:54,779
What?

276
00:26:58,450 --> 00:27:00,660
I can't wait to see the new styles.

277
00:27:00,744 --> 00:27:01,661
Found it.

278
00:27:04,331 --> 00:27:08,084
When you go to Ginza Boutique,
ask for Mr. Hashimoto, not Mr. Kim.

279
00:27:09,044 --> 00:27:13,006
Mr. Kim can be awfully frustrating.
He never seems to understand.

280
00:27:13,089 --> 00:27:15,800
Joseon people are a little slow, you know.

281
00:27:17,218 --> 00:27:18,386
No offense.

282
00:27:18,970 --> 00:27:20,555
No worries. None taken.

283
00:27:28,229 --> 00:27:29,147
Oh no.

284
00:27:30,023 --> 00:27:31,024
What's wrong?

285
00:27:31,608 --> 00:27:35,487
It's confidential mail
meant for the inspector general to open,

286
00:27:36,154 --> 00:27:37,530
but I opened it by mistake.

287
00:27:39,282 --> 00:27:40,241
What do I do?

288
00:27:41,284 --> 00:27:43,536
I mixed up these two envelopes.

289
00:27:44,663 --> 00:27:45,538
Oh no.

290
00:27:45,622 --> 00:27:46,790
Oh no.

291
00:27:52,921 --> 00:27:53,880
Myung-nam.

292
00:27:53,963 --> 00:27:55,340
I'm so sorry.

293
00:28:00,178 --> 00:28:01,513
Thank you.

294
00:28:01,596 --> 00:28:06,101
Oh dear. It seems my daughter was a bit
taken aback by the talk of marriage.

295
00:28:07,477 --> 00:28:09,104
The truth is,

296
00:28:09,187 --> 00:28:12,440
I lured her here by promising <i>tonkatsu</i>
on Bonjeong Avenue.

297
00:28:13,858 --> 00:28:16,653
I called Hideo here without telling him
what this was about, either.

298
00:28:16,736 --> 00:28:17,904
Oh dear.

299
00:28:20,782 --> 00:28:24,285
This feels like the first chapter
of a popular fiction.

300
00:28:29,082 --> 00:28:29,916
Goodness.

301
00:28:31,334 --> 00:28:32,669
Oh my.

302
00:28:34,629 --> 00:28:35,463
Yamada!

303
00:28:37,674 --> 00:28:39,843
Did he just call me?

304
00:28:39,926 --> 00:28:40,802
Yes.

305
00:28:42,262 --> 00:28:43,680
What am I going to do?

306
00:28:44,764 --> 00:28:48,268
I'll sort this out.
You'd better go on inside.

307
00:28:48,351 --> 00:28:50,645
- So he won't get angrier later.
- Okay.

308
00:28:54,816 --> 00:28:55,817
You got this.

309
00:28:57,193 --> 00:28:58,027
Thank you.

310
00:29:05,493 --> 00:29:07,120
Did you call for me?

311
00:29:24,345 --> 00:29:26,014
1919
THE KYEAM-RI RIOT

312
00:29:26,097 --> 00:29:28,099
OFFICIAL CASUALTY LIST

313
00:29:30,059 --> 00:29:31,603
Keum Seo-hee…

314
00:29:35,440 --> 00:29:36,483
Keum Seo-hee…

315
00:29:36,566 --> 00:29:38,318
Not here.

316
00:29:40,069 --> 00:29:41,196
Ms. Keum?

317
00:29:43,990 --> 00:29:45,450
Why are you here?

318
00:29:48,620 --> 00:29:49,579
Mr. Sato.

319
00:29:51,956 --> 00:29:53,625
Were you in there?

320
00:29:53,708 --> 00:29:54,542
Yes.

321
00:29:55,210 --> 00:29:56,419
What are you doing here?

322
00:29:57,837 --> 00:30:00,256
I'm running a quick errand.

323
00:30:00,757 --> 00:30:02,509
A quick errand?

324
00:30:02,592 --> 00:30:04,928
Yes, but I'm done now.

325
00:30:06,805 --> 00:30:07,722
Hold on.

326
00:30:09,933 --> 00:30:10,809
Stay right there.

327
00:30:11,559 --> 00:30:13,061
Your hand behind your back.

328
00:30:20,068 --> 00:30:21,319
What is that…

329
00:30:24,697 --> 00:30:25,949
in your hand?

330
00:30:29,953 --> 00:30:30,829
Can you

331
00:30:32,539 --> 00:30:33,623
show me your hand?

332
00:31:01,401 --> 00:31:04,445
Well, I gave her that chocolate.

333
00:31:05,613 --> 00:31:08,116
Please don't tell the inspector general.

334
00:31:08,199 --> 00:31:09,701
I'm asking you, please.

335
00:31:20,879 --> 00:31:24,048
I really appreciate you
taking the time today.

336
00:31:25,008 --> 00:31:27,260
- Well then.
- Yes, I'll be in touch.

337
00:31:27,343 --> 00:31:28,595
- I'll see you again.
- Of course.

338
00:31:34,100 --> 00:31:35,143
Hideo.

339
00:31:35,226 --> 00:31:36,811
Show Mr. Kim out.

340
00:31:38,062 --> 00:31:38,897
Yes, sir.

341
00:31:38,980 --> 00:31:40,773
All right, Myung-nam. Say goodbye.

342
00:31:42,567 --> 00:31:43,401
Goodbye.

343
00:31:43,484 --> 00:31:45,320
We'll be on our way, then.

344
00:31:45,403 --> 00:31:46,696
Take care.

345
00:31:47,488 --> 00:31:48,323
Let's go.

346
00:31:55,163 --> 00:31:56,623
Aren't you going to clean the table?

347
00:31:57,957 --> 00:31:58,958
Right away, sir.

348
00:31:59,542 --> 00:32:01,544
Is there any new mail?

349
00:32:02,545 --> 00:32:03,755
Yes,

350
00:32:03,838 --> 00:32:04,964
there is.

351
00:32:18,811 --> 00:32:20,355
I'm sorry.

352
00:32:20,438 --> 00:32:24,442
I thought it was addressed to me
and opened it by accident!

353
00:32:24,525 --> 00:32:25,944
Please forgive me.

354
00:32:31,199 --> 00:32:32,533
Did you take out what was inside?

355
00:32:33,660 --> 00:32:35,370
I only opened it. I swear I never looked.

356
00:32:35,453 --> 00:32:37,038
You opened the envelope,

357
00:32:38,873 --> 00:32:40,249
but you didn't look inside?

358
00:32:40,333 --> 00:32:41,542
I swear.

359
00:32:41,626 --> 00:32:42,752
I never looked.

360
00:32:43,670 --> 00:32:45,338
I'm terribly sorry.

361
00:32:53,638 --> 00:32:55,932
NA BYEONG-SUN

362
00:32:56,015 --> 00:32:58,601
KEUM SEOK-JUNG

363
00:33:38,016 --> 00:33:39,559
Sorry, madam.

364
00:33:40,727 --> 00:33:42,729
It seemed like
you didn't have a photo of Seo-hee.

365
00:33:45,648 --> 00:33:48,568
I never had a picture of my mom,

366
00:33:49,652 --> 00:33:51,988
so I can barely remember her face.

367
00:34:11,174 --> 00:34:12,633
When did I get this?

368
00:34:13,843 --> 00:34:17,430
Thanks for walking us out. See you soon.

369
00:34:17,513 --> 00:34:18,347
All right.

370
00:34:19,766 --> 00:34:20,600
Go on.

371
00:34:39,911 --> 00:34:40,995
Inspector Matsui.

372
00:34:41,662 --> 00:34:43,539
This is Sato, the Inspector General.

373
00:34:44,373 --> 00:34:47,001
I want the full list
of the materials you sent over.

374
00:34:59,847 --> 00:35:01,349
A family photo?

375
00:35:04,185 --> 00:35:05,478
Inspector General.

376
00:35:05,561 --> 00:35:06,979
It's Interpreter Sato.

377
00:35:07,063 --> 00:35:09,065
I see. That'll be all.

378
00:35:32,380 --> 00:35:33,214
Help yourself.

379
00:35:34,549 --> 00:35:35,550
No, thank you.

380
00:35:39,595 --> 00:35:40,805
That was out of nowhere.

381
00:35:41,514 --> 00:35:42,431
A marriage?

382
00:35:43,975 --> 00:35:46,853
The wedding will be held
once the League of Nations Commission

383
00:35:46,936 --> 00:35:48,813
finishes their visit to Keijo this summer.

384
00:35:50,022 --> 00:35:53,317
Mr. Kim plans to hand Joil Textile Factory
over to you within three years.

385
00:35:53,401 --> 00:35:55,570
Once you're married,
move over to Joil Textile Factory.

386
00:35:56,070 --> 00:35:58,072
What good is sitting around
as an interpreter?

387
00:35:58,156 --> 00:36:00,366
You're only wasting your time.

388
00:36:00,449 --> 00:36:03,786
Is this about what's best for me?

389
00:36:04,912 --> 00:36:06,080
Isn't it for yourself?

390
00:36:06,164 --> 00:36:07,999
Don't be so cynical.

391
00:36:10,168 --> 00:36:12,795
A marriage like this is only natural
for someone in your position.

392
00:36:13,296 --> 00:36:15,298
We each have something the other needs.

393
00:36:15,381 --> 00:36:17,216
We exchange what's needed.

394
00:36:19,552 --> 00:36:23,431
Surely your marriage wasn't based
on a bargain like that.

395
00:36:25,266 --> 00:36:26,100
That's right.

396
00:36:27,560 --> 00:36:29,395
And I paid a heavy price for that.

397
00:36:35,902 --> 00:36:37,486
After that failure,

398
00:36:38,321 --> 00:36:42,074
I chose my next marriage based on
what I needed and what I hoped to achieve.

399
00:36:42,783 --> 00:36:44,744
That's how I came to where I am today.

400
00:36:46,996 --> 00:36:47,914
I have no regrets.

401
00:36:52,793 --> 00:36:53,961
I guess you regret

402
00:36:55,588 --> 00:36:56,714
marrying Mother, then.

403
00:36:58,049 --> 00:36:58,883
No.

404
00:36:59,675 --> 00:37:01,677
It was a failure, but I don't regret it.

405
00:37:01,761 --> 00:37:03,930
I've never regretted my choices.

406
00:37:06,057 --> 00:37:07,642
Even if I could turn back time,

407
00:37:08,392 --> 00:37:10,019
I'd make the same choice.

408
00:37:11,896 --> 00:37:14,232
The same goes
for making you my son, Hideo.

409
00:37:19,987 --> 00:37:20,947
You hurt your hand.

410
00:37:22,907 --> 00:37:24,033
You should be more careful.

411
00:37:26,744 --> 00:37:28,120
Just as I have no regrets,

412
00:37:31,624 --> 00:37:33,042
I hope you won't either.

413
00:37:49,350 --> 00:37:51,394
Why were you at the Government-General?

414
00:37:52,144 --> 00:37:54,230
Father wants me
to marry the inspector general's son.

415
00:37:56,774 --> 00:37:57,650
You startled me.

416
00:37:59,110 --> 00:38:01,112
I'm sorry, there was a puppy on the road…

417
00:38:06,534 --> 00:38:08,244
By the way, what did you just say?

418
00:38:09,870 --> 00:38:10,830
You're getting married?

419
00:38:11,914 --> 00:38:13,749
Yes, I am.

420
00:38:25,678 --> 00:38:27,054
SUWON POLICE STATION

421
00:38:27,138 --> 00:38:29,724
Are these from Suwon Police Station?

422
00:38:30,433 --> 00:38:32,685
There's nothing out of the ordinary.

423
00:38:35,896 --> 00:38:37,732
Keum Seok-jung is dead.

424
00:38:37,815 --> 00:38:39,400
His daughter died back then.

425
00:38:40,693 --> 00:38:42,236
Find out where his wife is.

426
00:38:42,320 --> 00:38:44,196
If she's dead,
find out when and how she died.

427
00:38:45,489 --> 00:38:46,324
Yes, sir.

428
00:38:49,785 --> 00:38:51,704
So about Keum Seok-jung's interrogation…

429
00:38:55,166 --> 00:38:56,000
Yes?

430
00:38:56,083 --> 00:38:59,253
He asked specifically
for Keum Seo-hee to interpret.

431
00:39:00,671 --> 00:39:01,505
Yes, he did.

432
00:39:04,925 --> 00:39:07,887
Look into Keum Seo-hee's record.
Her past, all of it. Leave nothing out.

433
00:39:09,263 --> 00:39:10,097
Yes, sir!

434
00:39:14,143 --> 00:39:15,436
RELIEF FOR KIDS' CONSTIPATION

435
00:39:15,519 --> 00:39:16,562
Kkot-nim.

436
00:39:17,396 --> 00:39:19,190
Why aren't you taking that?

437
00:39:19,273 --> 00:39:22,151
Ga-kyung gave this to me.

438
00:39:23,027 --> 00:39:24,236
I want to save it.

439
00:39:24,987 --> 00:39:25,988
Don't be silly.

440
00:39:26,072 --> 00:39:27,573
Medicine is meant to be taken.

441
00:39:28,366 --> 00:39:29,283
Use it or lose it.

442
00:39:29,367 --> 00:39:30,326
Geum-ok.

443
00:39:30,910 --> 00:39:31,786
Hey, Mr. Lee!

444
00:39:31,869 --> 00:39:33,662
Hello.

445
00:39:33,746 --> 00:39:34,830
Kkot-nim is here too.

446
00:39:35,706 --> 00:39:36,540
Perfect.

447
00:39:39,251 --> 00:39:40,211
Have some potatoes.

448
00:39:40,294 --> 00:39:41,295
Oh my.

449
00:39:43,172 --> 00:39:45,132
Goodness, thank you.

450
00:39:45,216 --> 00:39:47,176
Wow, thank you.

451
00:39:47,259 --> 00:39:48,719
Where'd you get the potatoes?

452
00:39:49,303 --> 00:39:50,721
Did you go haul a load?

453
00:39:50,805 --> 00:39:51,680
Yeah.

454
00:39:51,764 --> 00:39:54,642
I had no work all week
and finally hauled one load today.

455
00:39:57,186 --> 00:39:58,020
This damn leg.

456
00:39:59,605 --> 00:40:02,066
I walked just a little,
and it's acting up again.

457
00:40:02,149 --> 00:40:05,111
You earned that wound
fighting for our country. Look after it.

458
00:40:08,656 --> 00:40:10,366
Kkot-nim, eat before it gets cold.

459
00:40:10,449 --> 00:40:11,617
So hot!

460
00:40:11,700 --> 00:40:12,952
Come on, you silly goose.

461
00:40:13,494 --> 00:40:14,537
Blow on it first.

462
00:40:19,250 --> 00:40:20,501
You should have some too.

463
00:40:23,671 --> 00:40:25,589
I haven't had potatoes in a long time.

464
00:40:25,673 --> 00:40:26,799
- Potatoes.
- Good grief!

465
00:40:29,343 --> 00:40:31,637
When did you get here?

466
00:40:32,263 --> 00:40:34,598
My baby is hungry.

467
00:40:34,682 --> 00:40:35,808
Just give me one.

468
00:40:38,727 --> 00:40:39,979
Hey. Here.

469
00:40:40,062 --> 00:40:40,896
Have this.

470
00:40:45,484 --> 00:40:50,489
It's hot, sweetie.
Mommy will blow on it for you.

471
00:40:55,202 --> 00:40:57,705
Nom, nom.

472
00:40:58,414 --> 00:40:59,582
Bun-i!

473
00:41:00,291 --> 00:41:02,835
If you eat it all up, what about me?

474
00:41:10,259 --> 00:41:12,636
Mommy needs to eat to make milk.

475
00:41:18,767 --> 00:41:20,978
Right. You need to eat too.

476
00:41:21,061 --> 00:41:24,899
Did Mrs. Jo-i's baby really die?

477
00:41:24,982 --> 00:41:25,816
Yes.

478
00:41:27,193 --> 00:41:29,904
She was starving
and had no milk to feed her baby.

479
00:41:31,155 --> 00:41:32,948
That baby wrap she was holding

480
00:41:33,532 --> 00:41:35,284
looked brand-new, didn't it?

481
00:41:35,367 --> 00:41:37,244
I'm sure Ga-kyung gave it to her.

482
00:41:37,953 --> 00:41:40,414
Kkot-nim got constipation medicine,
No-ma got a bicycle,

483
00:41:40,915 --> 00:41:42,833
I got these rubber shoes,

484
00:41:43,626 --> 00:41:45,169
and you got some salve.

485
00:41:45,252 --> 00:41:46,128
My goodness.

486
00:41:47,213 --> 00:41:49,798
She left something for everyone,
before moving so far away.

487
00:41:49,882 --> 00:41:51,008
What's the point?

488
00:41:51,675 --> 00:41:53,552
She left without even saying goodbye.

489
00:41:55,429 --> 00:41:57,139
She's gone so far away to America.

490
00:41:57,765 --> 00:41:59,475
We may never get to see her again.

491
00:42:01,519 --> 00:42:02,645
Ga-kyung

492
00:42:03,521 --> 00:42:05,439
will be all right in America, won't she?

493
00:42:05,523 --> 00:42:06,398
Of course.

494
00:42:07,399 --> 00:42:10,110
She'd find her way out of a tiger's den.

495
00:42:13,405 --> 00:42:16,116
I miss Ga-kyung.

496
00:42:27,878 --> 00:42:28,879
You're here?

497
00:42:34,843 --> 00:42:35,678
I'm sorry.

498
00:42:37,471 --> 00:42:38,305
What?

499
00:42:39,682 --> 00:42:41,141
I misunderstood earlier.

500
00:42:41,225 --> 00:42:43,352
We were at the Inspector General's Office.

501
00:42:44,311 --> 00:42:45,271
Is that what you mean?

502
00:42:46,021 --> 00:42:47,439
Don't worry about it.

503
00:42:47,523 --> 00:42:51,652
I'm used to being the first suspect
whenever something goes missing.

504
00:42:55,364 --> 00:42:56,198
I'm sorry.

505
00:42:58,284 --> 00:42:59,243
Ms. Keum.

506
00:43:01,912 --> 00:43:02,788
I trust you.

507
00:43:03,914 --> 00:43:04,957
I don't suspect you.

508
00:43:08,794 --> 00:43:09,628
Well…

509
00:43:14,758 --> 00:43:15,801
Let's go.

510
00:43:20,306 --> 00:43:23,684
Today, I'm having
the most expensive meal in Gyeongseong.

511
00:43:23,767 --> 00:43:25,603
I'm going to clean out your wallet.

512
00:43:26,103 --> 00:43:28,480
Haven't you already cleaned out my wallet?

513
00:43:29,064 --> 00:43:29,898
What?

514
00:43:30,524 --> 00:43:32,651
I have no idea what you're talking about.

515
00:43:34,028 --> 00:43:36,238
Please, sir.
I can run faster than a bicycle.

516
00:43:36,322 --> 00:43:37,781
Gosh, you idiot.

517
00:43:39,992 --> 00:43:43,203
Hey, you punk. What kind of delivery boy
doesn't have a bicycle?

518
00:43:46,665 --> 00:43:48,083
Stay here for a minute.

519
00:43:49,209 --> 00:43:50,210
Where are you going?

520
00:43:50,794 --> 00:43:51,795
I know him.

521
00:43:52,379 --> 00:43:53,213
Sorry?

522
00:43:53,756 --> 00:43:54,632
Wait, how…

523
00:43:55,633 --> 00:43:57,051
How do you know him?

524
00:43:57,134 --> 00:43:59,470
He's the brother of a kid I'm tutoring.

525
00:43:59,553 --> 00:44:01,347
Tutoring?

526
00:44:01,430 --> 00:44:02,848
But what kind of tutoring?

527
00:44:02,931 --> 00:44:03,766
Please, just…

528
00:44:05,726 --> 00:44:07,019
I think he's hurt. One moment.

529
00:44:08,604 --> 00:44:09,605
Don't go.

530
00:44:11,815 --> 00:44:13,692
He'll be embarrassed
if you see him like that.

531
00:44:14,860 --> 00:44:15,694
What?

532
00:44:15,778 --> 00:44:19,114
Have you no shame? Isn't this too much?

533
00:44:19,198 --> 00:44:20,824
Whatever, I don't care.

534
00:44:20,908 --> 00:44:22,534
Just get out of here.

535
00:44:22,618 --> 00:44:24,161
- Sir.
- Go away.

536
00:44:26,872 --> 00:44:27,915
Come on, let's go.

537
00:44:32,544 --> 00:44:33,545
Wait, this won't do.

538
00:44:33,629 --> 00:44:34,922
Mr. Sato.

539
00:44:35,005 --> 00:44:35,881
Listen.

540
00:44:36,507 --> 00:44:39,593
Stay away from those kinds of people.
Don't take pity on them.

541
00:44:40,302 --> 00:44:41,136
Excuse me?

542
00:44:41,220 --> 00:44:44,640
You work for the Government-General,
yet you'd help a poor Joseon man?

543
00:44:44,723 --> 00:44:47,017
Do you think your father would be happy
if he found out?

544
00:44:47,601 --> 00:44:48,435
Ms. Keum.

545
00:44:49,144 --> 00:44:51,897
He's way beneath you, you know.

546
00:44:52,648 --> 00:44:55,234
So keep your distance. Stay away from him.

547
00:44:55,317 --> 00:44:57,361
Don't get involved with him again, okay?

548
00:45:01,532 --> 00:45:04,243
You sound more like
a Government-General official than I do.

549
00:45:05,369 --> 00:45:06,203
What?

550
00:45:06,829 --> 00:45:07,788
You weren't

551
00:45:08,455 --> 00:45:11,250
any different from him
before you joined the Government-General.

552
00:45:11,333 --> 00:45:12,668
Neither was I.

553
00:45:12,751 --> 00:45:15,129
Have you already forgotten those days?
He's beneath me?

554
00:45:16,672 --> 00:45:18,757
You've adapted perfectly
to the Government-General.

555
00:45:19,425 --> 00:45:20,676
Well, that figures.

556
00:45:20,759 --> 00:45:23,011
I suppose you're clever enough
to have caught

557
00:45:24,263 --> 00:45:25,431
Mr. Yoo's eye.

558
00:45:28,142 --> 00:45:32,104
Well, what do you know about me?

559
00:45:34,022 --> 00:45:36,191
And why am I the only one
getting the blame?

560
00:45:36,275 --> 00:45:39,027
You came from the streets yourself,

561
00:45:39,111 --> 00:45:41,613
but you've adapted perfectly
as a Japanese now.

562
00:45:43,365 --> 00:45:44,658
You know what?

563
00:45:44,742 --> 00:45:47,703
An elite from Tokyo Imperial University
and the inspector general's son

564
00:45:47,786 --> 00:45:49,663
playing teacher to some poor Joseon kid.

565
00:45:49,747 --> 00:45:50,873
That's so obnoxious.

566
00:45:59,006 --> 00:45:59,840
That's enough.

567
00:46:04,553 --> 00:46:05,387
I'm sorry.

568
00:46:07,473 --> 00:46:08,974
I just…

569
00:46:09,850 --> 00:46:12,352
I just wanted you to be careful.

570
00:46:12,436 --> 00:46:13,395
Forget it.

571
00:46:14,229 --> 00:46:15,814
Thanks for the reality check.

572
00:46:16,815 --> 00:46:18,567
Wait, that's not what I meant.

573
00:46:18,650 --> 00:46:19,526
Let's have dinner

574
00:46:20,652 --> 00:46:21,487
some other time.

575
00:46:23,530 --> 00:46:24,656
I'll see you tomorrow.

576
00:46:27,451 --> 00:46:28,535
No, wait.

577
00:46:34,374 --> 00:46:36,168
Darn it, this is driving me crazy.

578
00:46:36,251 --> 00:46:38,045
How do they know each other?

579
00:46:41,215 --> 00:46:44,259
And how did he get his bicycle stolen?

580
00:46:46,011 --> 00:46:46,970
It's bugging me.

581
00:46:51,308 --> 00:46:53,227
And why does he look so sad,

582
00:46:53,310 --> 00:46:55,354
trudging along like a drenched puppy?

583
00:46:55,437 --> 00:46:56,772
He's making me feel awful.

584
00:47:08,325 --> 00:47:09,201
What?

585
00:47:10,244 --> 00:47:11,703
You'll marry Sato Hideo?

586
00:47:12,496 --> 00:47:13,330
Yes.

587
00:47:13,413 --> 00:47:14,623
Getting married?

588
00:47:15,332 --> 00:47:17,251
To a guy like Sato of all people?

589
00:47:17,334 --> 00:47:18,877
Do you realize how dangerous that is?

590
00:47:20,337 --> 00:47:21,755
Absolutely not.

591
00:47:22,339 --> 00:47:25,843
It's not as though I can just say no
to this marriage.

592
00:47:27,636 --> 00:47:30,597
The wedding will take place once
the foreign commission leaves this summer.

593
00:47:30,681 --> 00:47:33,725
I don't want to marry him,
but if I can't avoid it,

594
00:47:34,309 --> 00:47:36,353
I'll try to see what I can do later.

595
00:47:39,815 --> 00:47:42,025
Why are we fighting for independence?

596
00:47:44,653 --> 00:47:46,989
Why have we all come together?

597
00:47:48,031 --> 00:47:51,577
So that we can all live with dignity
on our own land.

598
00:47:51,660 --> 00:47:53,370
But independence can't come

599
00:47:53,453 --> 00:47:55,914
at the cost of destroying your life.

600
00:47:58,125 --> 00:47:59,459
- But…
- Myung-nam.

601
00:48:01,086 --> 00:48:02,379
In the past, we've…

602
00:48:09,303 --> 00:48:10,220
Never mind.

603
00:48:10,804 --> 00:48:12,014
In the past?

604
00:48:13,807 --> 00:48:16,310
What happened in the past?

605
00:48:21,064 --> 00:48:23,317
If Sato dies,
the marriage will be called off.

606
00:48:26,570 --> 00:48:29,031
It was only arranged because
he's the inspector general.

607
00:48:29,948 --> 00:48:30,782
You mean…

608
00:48:35,370 --> 00:48:36,204
Okay.

609
00:48:36,705 --> 00:48:39,166
I'll use my position
as Sato Hideo's fiancée,

610
00:48:39,249 --> 00:48:40,751
and see what I can do for now.

611
00:48:42,502 --> 00:48:43,337
Okay.

612
00:48:45,797 --> 00:48:46,632
By the way,

613
00:48:47,341 --> 00:48:49,426
what happened in the past?

614
00:48:50,636 --> 00:48:53,847
Last time, you were telling us something,
but you never finished.

615
00:49:04,316 --> 00:49:05,984
Years ago, we…

616
00:49:13,700 --> 00:49:16,745
<i>I put someone's life in danger.</i>

617
00:49:20,290 --> 00:49:23,585
<i>And I put the person she loved</i>
<i>through hell.</i>

618
00:49:27,965 --> 00:49:28,799
What's that?

619
00:49:32,928 --> 00:49:34,513
Sato Hideo brought it.

620
00:49:34,596 --> 00:49:36,306
Sato Hideo?

621
00:49:36,390 --> 00:49:37,307
Yes.

622
00:49:37,391 --> 00:49:38,976
He comes by every so often.

623
00:49:39,059 --> 00:49:41,186
He asks me to take good care of Mr. Suzuki

624
00:49:41,269 --> 00:49:43,605
since he has no family to visit him.

625
00:49:44,272 --> 00:49:46,984
I guess he worries about him
since they worked together

626
00:49:47,067 --> 00:49:48,151
even if only briefly.

627
00:49:51,363 --> 00:49:52,990
It's time for rounds. Let's go.

628
00:49:53,073 --> 00:49:54,032
All right.

629
00:49:55,492 --> 00:49:57,452
Suzuki?

630
00:50:00,205 --> 00:50:02,290
- Suzuki, can you hear me?
- Mr. Suzuki.

631
00:50:05,043 --> 00:50:07,045
Is my name still

632
00:50:08,255 --> 00:50:09,464
Suzuki?

633
00:50:12,009 --> 00:50:12,843
Come on.

634
00:50:14,344 --> 00:50:17,681
I've been to the other side and back,

635
00:50:19,224 --> 00:50:21,476
and we still haven't won our independence?

636
00:50:51,965 --> 00:50:52,841
Mr. Sato!

637
00:51:04,686 --> 00:51:05,520
I'm sorry.

638
00:51:08,774 --> 00:51:10,567
I shouldn't have said that.

639
00:51:13,779 --> 00:51:14,613
I'm sorry.

640
00:51:23,080 --> 00:51:23,997
I missed

641
00:51:25,123 --> 00:51:25,957
the streetcar.

642
00:51:27,667 --> 00:51:29,878
I wanted to apologize.

643
00:51:31,171 --> 00:51:32,339
Will you accept

644
00:51:33,173 --> 00:51:35,467
my apology?

645
00:51:39,221 --> 00:51:41,473
I'll buy you a drink.

646
00:51:42,557 --> 00:51:43,433
Come with me.

647
00:51:46,728 --> 00:51:47,562
Let's go.

648
00:51:52,025 --> 00:51:52,859
No.

649
00:51:56,530 --> 00:51:57,572
It's my treat.

650
00:51:58,073 --> 00:51:59,074
I promised.

651
00:52:02,452 --> 00:52:03,620
Except…

652
00:52:04,412 --> 00:52:06,790
you didn't say "please."

653
00:52:06,873 --> 00:52:07,791
I'm sorry?

654
00:52:39,698 --> 00:52:40,615
Thank you.

655
00:52:43,034 --> 00:52:44,369
Oh my.

656
00:52:44,452 --> 00:52:47,747
Beer really is golden, just like I heard.

657
00:52:50,167 --> 00:52:53,211
<i>I was just going to buy him</i>
<i>a glass of rice wine.</i>

658
00:52:53,295 --> 00:52:55,589
Doesn't he ever take you out
to places like this?

659
00:52:55,672 --> 00:52:56,506
Sorry?

660
00:53:00,594 --> 00:53:03,388
Of course not.
He wouldn't bring me somewhere like this.

661
00:53:03,471 --> 00:53:06,558
You know, it'd look bad if people saw us.

662
00:53:07,517 --> 00:53:08,476
You know how it is.

663
00:53:17,694 --> 00:53:19,487
Thanks for the drink.

664
00:53:44,888 --> 00:53:47,057
Can I have one more glass?

665
00:54:12,832 --> 00:54:14,417
By the way,

666
00:54:15,210 --> 00:54:18,129
what's your Joseon name?

667
00:54:22,550 --> 00:54:24,970
I was just wondering.

668
00:54:25,720 --> 00:54:28,682
You must have had one before.

669
00:54:33,645 --> 00:54:35,230
Then let me ask something else.

670
00:54:35,855 --> 00:54:38,775
Why did you become an interpreter?

671
00:54:41,194 --> 00:54:43,405
Was it money, like it was for me?

672
00:54:45,198 --> 00:54:48,618
You can be honest with me.
It's nothing to hide.

673
00:54:52,956 --> 00:54:55,125
I don't belong anywhere anyway.

674
00:54:58,628 --> 00:55:00,046
Even with a gun in my hands,

675
00:55:02,007 --> 00:55:04,259
I wouldn't know which way to point it.

676
00:55:12,475 --> 00:55:13,310
Why?

677
00:55:14,102 --> 00:55:16,271
Are you disappointed
that I'm not in it for the money

678
00:55:16,354 --> 00:55:17,272
like you are?

679
00:55:17,355 --> 00:55:18,481
Yes.

680
00:55:20,859 --> 00:55:24,362
I thought you and I
had something in common.

681
00:55:25,864 --> 00:55:27,365
I guess I was wrong.

682
00:55:27,449 --> 00:55:30,910
Someone like you who lives in luxury
and a starving nobody like me

683
00:55:30,994 --> 00:55:33,038
could never be equals.

684
00:55:36,916 --> 00:55:39,127
And what's so terrible about money?

685
00:55:40,170 --> 00:55:42,547
At least money doesn't lie.

686
00:55:42,630 --> 00:55:45,759
Whether it's Japanese, Joseon,
or American money,

687
00:55:45,842 --> 00:55:49,387
once it's in your pocket,
it buys you food just the same.

688
00:55:57,062 --> 00:55:57,937
There was a time

689
00:55:59,981 --> 00:56:04,027
when I had no idea
where I belonged either.

690
00:56:06,863 --> 00:56:08,156
Whenever I felt that way…

691
00:56:10,408 --> 00:56:11,576
<i>Mom.</i>

692
00:56:12,243 --> 00:56:15,580
I was playing with the other kids today,

693
00:56:16,414 --> 00:56:21,336
and some man called us Joseon pigs.

694
00:56:23,171 --> 00:56:24,089
He did?

695
00:56:25,131 --> 00:56:26,091
Yes.

696
00:56:26,174 --> 00:56:32,972
I try not to think about it,
but I can't stop thinking about it.

697
00:56:35,225 --> 00:56:38,228
When that happens, just keep walking.

698
00:56:38,937 --> 00:56:40,563
Keep walking?

699
00:56:44,192 --> 00:56:48,446
Because where your feet stand is more real
than what's in your mind.

700
00:56:50,490 --> 00:56:52,700
The ground beneath your feet

701
00:56:53,660 --> 00:56:54,744
is yours,

702
00:56:56,079 --> 00:56:57,288
and that's your path.

703
00:57:01,584 --> 00:57:04,421
You don't know who your father is, do you?

704
00:57:05,088 --> 00:57:06,965
I bet you've got Jap blood in you too.

705
00:57:11,136 --> 00:57:12,429
<i>Whenever that happened,</i>

706
00:57:13,096 --> 00:57:15,473
<i>I just kept walking.</i>

707
00:57:17,016 --> 00:57:18,726
<i>Because where my feet stand</i>

708
00:57:19,269 --> 00:57:22,147
<i>is more real than what's in my mind.</i>

709
00:57:28,653 --> 00:57:32,031
So I kept walking and walking.

710
00:57:33,366 --> 00:57:35,952
But there was no path
and nothing in sight.

711
00:57:38,413 --> 00:57:39,414
Then…

712
00:58:23,082 --> 00:58:25,710
<i>just when I really felt like giving up…</i>

713
00:59:20,848 --> 00:59:23,268
<i>I finally saw the path.</i>

714
00:59:24,644 --> 00:59:26,187
What was it?

715
00:59:27,313 --> 00:59:28,940
Going to America.

716
00:59:30,900 --> 00:59:33,695
There is the tallest building
in the world.

717
00:59:33,778 --> 00:59:36,155
They said I could climb
all the way to the top.

718
00:59:36,239 --> 00:59:38,449
So I made up my mind to go there.

719
00:59:40,118 --> 00:59:43,037
I'm going to make it to America.

720
00:59:43,663 --> 00:59:45,999
I'll save enough money to go to America

721
00:59:46,082 --> 00:59:48,751
and climb all the way
to the top of the building.

722
00:59:52,213 --> 00:59:53,298
And then what?

723
00:59:54,215 --> 00:59:55,174
What?

724
01:00:00,638 --> 01:00:01,806
Once you get up there,

725
01:00:02,890 --> 01:00:04,017
what will you do next?

726
01:00:04,809 --> 01:00:05,768
Well…

727
01:00:09,647 --> 01:00:10,690
I don't know.

728
01:00:11,816 --> 01:00:14,485
I guess I'll find out when I get there.

729
01:00:15,111 --> 01:00:18,448
You won't know where a path leads
until you take it.

730
01:00:22,327 --> 01:00:23,703
So,

731
01:00:24,329 --> 01:00:25,663
you should

732
01:00:26,998 --> 01:00:29,584
take one step forward.

733
01:00:30,752 --> 01:00:32,253
Who knows?

734
01:00:33,087 --> 01:00:35,632
You might discover what you really want.

735
01:00:39,093 --> 01:00:40,261
Never mind.

736
01:00:40,887 --> 01:00:43,681
Oh, come on.

737
01:00:45,058 --> 01:00:47,143
Now, just do as I say.

738
01:00:47,226 --> 01:00:50,772
First, close your eyes and count to three.

739
01:00:50,855 --> 01:00:52,523
Then open them,

740
01:00:52,607 --> 01:00:55,610
and whatever you see first,
make that your goal.

741
01:00:56,653 --> 01:00:58,029
You should try it sometime.

742
01:00:58,112 --> 01:01:01,324
You have to pay for everything
except breathing in this world.

743
01:01:01,407 --> 01:01:04,118
This is one thing you can do for free.
You just need to blink.

744
01:01:34,023 --> 01:01:37,193
I'm not making it up. It's true.

745
01:01:40,530 --> 01:01:41,364
Yes.

746
01:01:43,991 --> 01:01:45,159
I suppose you're right.

747
01:01:47,662 --> 01:01:49,414
What?

748
01:01:50,248 --> 01:01:51,999
I can't hear you with all this noise.

749
01:02:01,634 --> 01:02:02,719
It's Kim Tae-ung.

750
01:02:05,680 --> 01:02:06,848
My Joseon name.

751
01:02:59,066 --> 01:03:02,987
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

752
01:03:03,070 --> 01:03:06,532
<i>She might've told Sato Hideo</i>
<i>things she shouldn't have while drunk.</i>

753
01:03:06,616 --> 01:03:08,242
<i>I don't understand…</i>

754
01:03:10,495 --> 01:03:11,621
<i>Mr. Sato!</i>

755
01:03:11,704 --> 01:03:15,708
I think Sato Hideo
has feelings for Seo-hee.

756
01:03:15,792 --> 01:03:19,420
<i>There'll be several senior officials</i>
<i>from the Government-General that day.</i>

757
01:03:19,504 --> 01:03:22,590
<i>We can't afford to miss this opportunity.</i>

758
01:03:22,673 --> 01:03:24,759
<i>You'll set off a bomb at the banquet?</i>

759
01:03:26,177 --> 01:03:28,721
No. You'll never get a bomb inside.

760
01:03:31,724 --> 01:03:34,101
<i>So in the end,</i>
<i>you chose to do something stupid.</i>

761
01:03:40,316 --> 01:03:42,318
Subtitle translation by: Youji Kim
